  "josekiIntro": "Here are some common corner joseki and the ideas behind them. Rather than memorizing the sequence, understanding why each move is played will serve you better in an actual game.",
  "joseki1Name": "Hoshi Joseki (Attach-and-Extend)",
  "joseki1Moves": "Black 1: Hoshi → White 2: Attach → Black 3: Hane → White 4: Extend → Black 5: Extend",
  "joseki1Idea": "The most basic joseki, simply dividing the border between each side's territory. Easy for beginners to play, and rarely causes life-and-death trouble in the corner.",
  "joseki2Name": "Komoku High Approach",
  "joseki2Moves": "Black 1: Komoku → White 2: High one-space approach → Black 3: Pincer or calm response",
  "joseki2Idea": "One of the standard approaches to a komoku. Whether to pincer or respond calmly changes the scale of the fight considerably, making it a key branching point for the opening strategy.",
  "joseki3Name": "Large Avalanche Joseki",
  "joseki3Moves": "Black 1: Komoku → White 2: Large knight's approach → Black 3: Large avalanche",
  "joseki3Idea": "A notoriously complex joseki with many variations. Rather than memorizing every line, understanding the overall aim — both sides probing each other's weaknesses — makes it easier to apply in a real game.",
  "joseki4Name": "One-Space Pincer Joseki",
  "joseki4Moves": "Black 1: Hoshi → White 2: Approach → Black 3: One-space pincer → White 4: 3-3 invasion",
  "joseki4Idea": "A joseki that starts a fight by pincering the approaching stone at close range. In exchange for giving the corner to White, Black builds outside thickness aimed at controlling the whole board.",
  "joseki5Name": "3-3 Invasion",
  "joseki5Moves": "Black: Hoshi → White: Invades the 3-3 point → Black: Blocks → White: Lives",
  "joseki5Idea": "A way to secure solid territory in the corner. Since White lives at the cost of giving Black outside thickness, how that thickness is used afterward becomes the key point.",

