  "howToPlayTitle": "How to Play",
  "boardPartsTitle": "Parts of the Board",
  "boardPartsBody": "The points where the board's lines cross are called \"intersections\" — this is where stones are placed. The board's edges are called \"sides\", the four corners are \"corners\", and the center of the board is the \"center point\" (tengen). On 9x9, 13x13, and 19x19 boards, certain intersections are marked with a slightly larger dot called a \"star point\" (hoshi) — the star point at the very center is specifically called the \"center point\". These terms are commonly used to describe positions during a game.",
  "placingStonesTitle": "Placing Stones",
  "placingStonesBody": "Starting with Black, players take turns placing stones on the intersections where the board's lines cross. Once a stone is placed, you cannot move it yourself.",
  "libertyAtariTitle": "Liberties and Atari",
  "libertyAtariBody": "The open intersections directly above, below, left, and right of a stone (or a connected group of stones) are called \"liberties\". When a stone has only one liberty left, it is said to be in \"atari\" — if the opponent plays next, that stone will be captured. In the diagram below, the stone on the left has 4 liberties, while the stone on the right is surrounded on 3 sides and is in atari (1 liberty).",
  "captureTitle": "Capturing Stones",
  "captureBody": "A stone (or group of stones) with 0 liberties is removed from the board. Captured stones are counted as \"captures\" in the final scoring.",
  "suicideMoveTitle": "Forbidden Move: Suicide",
  "suicideMoveBody": "As a rule, you cannot play a move that would leave your own stone (or group) with 0 liberties (the suicide rule). However, if that move captures an opponent's stones and results in your stone having liberties, it is allowed as an exception.",
  "territoryTitle": "Territory (Chinese Rules)",
  "territoryBody": "Goen uses Chinese scoring rules. The final score is calculated as \"number of your stones + territory you have surrounded\", and whoever has the higher number wins. Unlike Japanese rules, your own stones remaining on the board are counted directly toward your score.",
  "koRuleTitle": "Forbidden Move: Ko",
  "koRuleBody": "You cannot immediately recapture in a way that simply returns the board to the position from one move ago (the ko rule). You must play elsewhere first before you can recapture at that same point.",
  "endGameTitle": "End of Game",
  "endGameBody": "The game ends when both players pass in succession, and the winner is decided by counting territory. Goen has no timer, so feel free to take as long as you need to think it through.",
